; pcorner.macro - generalized corner XYZ probe for grblHAL (any of the 4 stock corners).
;
; Safe probing philosophy: probe Z down to DISCOVER the
; spoilboard and stock-top heights, never rapid to an assumed Z; decompose every reposition
; into an explicit safe-Z lift -> traverse -> descend; route axis-at-a-time around the corner.
;
; Drop at the root of the SD card / littlefs. Args are passed via GLOBALS set before a NO-ARG call (grblHAL's
; O-word CALL does not reliably accept multiple bracketed call args), then invoke:
;   #<_ls_corner> = <1-4>     1=FL 2=FR 3=BL 4=BR. Sets the probe-direction signs:
;                               sx = +1 for LEFT corners (1,3), -1 for RIGHT (2,4)   (probe toward the X face)
;                               sy = +1 for FRONT corners (1,2), -1 for BACK (3,4)   (probe toward the Y face)
;   #<_ls_refx>   = machine X of the reference, just OUTSIDE this corner's X face (clear air).
;   #<_ls_refy>   = machine Y of the reference, just OUTSIDE this corner's Y face.
;   #<_ls_rad>    = probe tip radius (mm).
;   #<_ls_spacer> = ACCEPTED AND IGNORED since the DISCOVER path was removed (2026-08-06). It only ever
;                   fed the spoilboard-derived floor. The caller now supplies #<_bottom> directly, so a
;                   sacrificial backer has to be folded into THAT value by the caller instead.
;   #<_ls_thickness> = estimated stock thickness (mm). The X/Y face probes themselves search at a FIXED
;                   5mm below the measured top now, not the material midpoint (see #<_start_z> below) - but
;                   this is used for the pre-probe APPROACH height (see #<_bottom> + this + #<_ls_plateoffset>
;                   below), so the machine doesn't rapid down further than the caller's own best estimate of
;                   where the top actually is. Revived 2026-07-27 - was genuinely unused between those two
;                   dates (see git history), when the approach height was a blind "assume <= 1in" constant.
;   #<_ls_topx>, #<_ls_topy>     = offset (mm) from the reference, into the stock, to the stock-top probe
;                   XY. Derived from the probing 3D probe's body diameter (StartJobView.BuildProgram) -
;                   not stored per fixture. Also reused as the edge probes' anchor (see below) - no separate
;                   edge offset is needed since the top-probe XY is already verified to be over solid stock.
;   #<_ls_searchf>= fast search feed (mm/min), from the probe definition (Probe/Search feed).
;   #<_ls_latchf> = slow latch / re-probe feed (mm/min), from the probe definition (Latch feed).
;   #<_ls_startz> = ACCEPTED, no longer selects anything (2026-08-06). It used to pick DISCOVER (>= 9000)
;                   vs REUSE; there is now ONE path. Still echoed in the PC ENTER trace so existing logs
;                   stay comparable. Every corner probes its OWN stock top, so each reports its own Z
;                   (that spread across four corners is the flatness measurement).
;   #<_ls_mode>   = 0 = 3D probe (default).
;                   1 = touch plate - probes by electrical continuity. Both modes now behave identically
;                       here; the distinction only mattered to the removed spoilboard probe, which a
;                       continuity probe could not run against a non-conductive board.
;   #<_ls_plateoffset> = Z (mm) subtracted from the probed top before it is reported as this corner's Z.
;                       0 for the 3D probe, and for a touch plate run directly against conductive stock
;                       (no plate, no offset). A separate metal touch plate against non-conductive stock
;                       sets this to the plate's own known thickness (ProbeDefinition.PlateThickness).
;   #<_ls_lipoffset> = XY (mm) added, toward the stock interior, to the X/Y face probe results before they
;                       are reported as this corner's X/Y. 0 for the 3D probe (it touches the real stock
;                       edge directly). A touch plate that registers via a lip hooked over the corner (lip
;                       resting on/against the stock, its own outer wall set back from the true edge by the
;                       lip's depth) probes ITS OWN wall, not the stock's - this shifts the result back to
;                       the true edge by that depth (ProbeDefinition.LipWidth). Applied the same direction,
;                       and folded into the same radius-compensation term, as #<rad> below.
;   #<_ls_edgemargin> = XY (mm) extra clearance, on top of #<_ls_lipoffset> and #<rad>, that the X/Y face-probe
;                       descent points back off before dropping to probe depth. lipoffset+rad alone is only
;                       exactly enough if the reference (#<rx>/#<ry>) sits EXACTLY at the true edge - but that
;                       reference is the CALLER's own estimate, not something this macro has confirmed yet
;                       (finding it is the whole point of the face probe about to run), so this pads it.
;                       FLOORED AT 20 by this macro (see the o13 block) - every caller in the app passes 10,
;                       and 10 was not enough on real hardware 2026-08-16: the reference sat ~7 mm inside the
;                       true face, leaving the Y-face start with ~4 mm of actual air instead of the intended
;                       11. A caller may ask for MORE; it cannot ask for less. A caller that omits it gets 0
;                       from grblHAL and is then floored to 20 like everyone else.
; The retreat clearance above a probed corner top (#<_safe_z> below) is a hardcoded 5 mm. It was 20, which
; is far more than any real spoilboard/stock variance - on a T-slot extrusion bed even 5 mm would take some
; doing - so 20 just spent Z travel on every retreat. A caller with a genuinely known safe height passes
; #<_ls_maxz>, which overrides the retreat entirely.
; DO NOT make this a caller-supplied global: tried #<_ls_topmargin> on 2026-08-02 and reading a global no
; caller had set threw "error:2 in named sub pcorner.macro" and aborted the run. Unlike the claim in
; #<_ls_edgemargin>'s note below, an unset global is NOT reliably 0 here. If this ever does need to be
; caller-driven, guard the read with EXISTS[] the way tc.macro's o199 does for #<_tlo_ref>.
;   #<_ls_maxz>   = OPTIONAL machine Z of a known-safe travel height, already trusted by the caller (e.g.
;                       the vise's own centre-of-stock probe, run before any corner calls). When > 0, this
;                       replaces BOTH the full retract to machine Z0 (o42 below - normally needed so the
;                       tool clears clamp/fence hardware between corners, which the vise has none of) and
;                       the pre-top-probe descent estimate (o43's #<_approach_z>) with this
;                       single known height - the tool never travels above it. 0/unset = old behavior
;                       (Corner Fence, no prior stock-top knowledge - safe but more conservative).
;   #<_ls_appz>   = OPTIONAL machine Z override for ONLY this call's very first height change (o48 below) -
;                       everything else this call still uses #<_ls_maxz>. For a corner reached via a detour
;                       that left the fixture's footprint entirely (Corner Fence's TLO-reference park at G30
;                       is the current use), so that one return crossing happens at Z0 (always safe) instead
;                       of #<_ls_maxz> (only trusted WITHIN the footprint), without losing the trusted height
;                       for the rest of this corner's own probe sequence. SENTINEL IS 9999 ("unset", not 0) -
;                       unlike #<_ls_maxz>, Z0 (machine top) is itself a legitimate override value here, so
;                       0 can't double as "no override".
;   #<_ls_inside> = 0 (default) = OUTSIDE corner/edge - approach from outside the stock, probing inward to
;                       find each face (the original/only behavior). 1 = INSIDE corner/edge - probe FROM
;                       inside a pocket/step OUTWARD to find each face instead (Start Job's "Dynamic" mode
;                       inside-corner/inside-edge picks). Implemented as a single sign flip on sx/sy right
;                       after they're derived from the corner id - every other line in this macro is already
;                       written purely in terms of sx/sy, so nothing else needs to change.
;   #<_ls_faces>  = 0 (default) = probe BOTH faces (a full corner - the original/only behavior). 1 = X face
;                       ONLY (an edge midpoint on an X-normal face - Start Job's "Dynamic" edge-midpoint
;                       picks use this to skip the other axis). 2 = Y face ONLY. The skipped axis's output
;                       (#<_corner_x> or #<_corner_y>) falls back to the un-probed top-probe anchor - callers
;                       doing a single-face pick must not treat that axis as a real touch result. >= 9000 = ignored, use #<_ls_maxz> instead.
;
; Outputs (GLOBAL named params, survive the SUB):
;   #<_corner_x> #<_corner_y>   radius-compensated corner XY (machine coords)
;   #<_corner_z>                THIS corner's stock-top Z (machine), less #<_ls_plateoffset> - varies
;                                per corner = flatness
;   #<_start_z>                 this corner's face-probe depth (top - 5, clamped to #<_ls_zfloor>)
;
; Preconditions: homed; probe on the main probe input ($6 polarity correct); stock >= 5 mm (the face probes
;   now seek at a FIXED 5mm below the measured top, not the material midpoint - thinner stock has its side
;   face searched below its own bottom) AND <= ~25 mm (1 in) thick - the stock-top probe now starts just
;   above 1 in for speed, so taller stock would be missed; Z probes seek to -140 (keep within $132). The
;   the reference must have clear air below it (no fence/clamp) and be >= body-radius
;   outside the faces.

O<pcorner> SUB
  #<rad>    = #1                 ; tip radius = the single CALL arg; rest via globals
  #<corner> = #<_ls_corner>
  #<rx>     = #<_ls_refx>
  #<ry>     = #<_ls_refy>
  #<sz_in>  = #<_ls_startz>
  #<mode>   = #<_ls_mode>        ; 0 = 3D probe, 1 = touch plate (see file header)
  #<maxz>   = #<_ls_maxz>        ; known-safe travel height (machine Z), 0/unset = no prior stock-top knowledge (see file header)
  #<appz>   = #<_ls_appz>        ; approach-only override for the FIRST height change below (see file header) - 0/unset = use #<maxz> same as every other height decision this call
  #<inside> = #<_ls_inside>      ; 0 = outside corner/edge (default), 1 = inside (see file header)
  #<faces>  = #<_ls_faces>       ; 0 = both faces (default), 1 = X face only, 2 = Y face only (see file header)
  #<edge_margin> = #<_ls_edgemargin>   ; extra face-probe descent clearance, caller-supplied (see file header)

  ; FLOOR the caller's margin. Every caller in the app passes 10, in four separate places - and 10 was
  ; not enough on real hardware 2026-08-16: the Y-face start ended up with ~4 mm of actual air, because
  ; the margin is measured from #<ry>, the operator's SAVED REFERENCE, and that reference sat ~7 mm
  ; inside the true face. This macro cannot know how far off the reference is (finding the face is the
  ; whole point of the probe about to run), so the only lever is more slop.
  ;
  ; Raised HERE rather than at the four call sites deliberately: one value, one place, and a caller that
  ; genuinely wants more can still ask for it. Costs nothing but travel - the G38.2 targets below are
  ; ABSOLUTE (#<rx> + 35*sx), so starting further out lengthens the seek instead of moving the goal.
  o13 IF [#<edge_margin> LT 20]
    #<edge_margin> = 20
  o13 ENDIF

  ; Clear G54 AND cancel any active G92 offset (AND TLO, belt-and-suspenders) so the absolute Z probes
  ; below run in TRUE machine coordinates. WCO = G5x offset + G92 offset + TLO (grblHAL gc_get_offset,
  ; gcode.c) - G10 L2 P1 alone only zeros the G5x term. Confirmed via a real Start Job failure + reading
  ; grblHAL's own report.c (is_g92_active()): a leftover non-zero G92 offset (TLO was already clean -
  ; $G showed G49) was the actual cause - a machine Z got driven as if it were a work Z offset by the
  ; stray G92, landing the search ~70mm from the real surface instead of the ~12mm it expects, well
  ; outside the search's travel cap. (The parameter in that incident, #<_ls_spoilz>, is gone with the
  ; spoilboard probe - the failure mode it demonstrates is not, which is why this zeroing stays.)
  ; Load Stock sets the real origin from the probed machine coordinates after all corners are done.
  G49
  G92.1
  G10 L2 P1 X0 Y0 Z0

  ; --- direction signs from the corner id ---
  o10 IF [[#<corner> EQ 1] OR [#<corner> EQ 3]]
    #<sx> = 1
  o10 ELSE
    #<sx> = -1
  o10 ENDIF
  o11 IF [[#<corner> EQ 1] OR [#<corner> EQ 2]]
    #<sy> = 1
  o11 ELSE
    #<sy> = -1
  o11 ENDIF
  ; Inside corner/edge (Dynamic mode): flip both signs so the approach comes FROM inside a pocket/step
  ; OUTWARD to each face, instead of from outside the stock inward. Everything below this point is already
  ; written purely in terms of sx/sy, so this single flip is the whole difference.
  o12 IF [#<inside> EQ 1]
    #<sx> = 0 - #<sx>
    #<sy> = 0 - #<sy>
  o12 ENDIF
  (PRINT, PC ENTER corner=#<corner> sx=#<sx> sy=#<sy> rad=#<rad> rx=#<rx> ry=#<ry> startz=#<sz_in> inside=#<inside> faces=#<faces>)
  ; Echo EVERY remaining input, here, before the first motion of the call. Two reasons this is worth the
  ; six lines. A probed corner that comes out wrong is otherwise un-diagnosable after the fact: the caller's
  ; parameters live in the sender and the log only showed the handful above, so "the macro did what it was
  ; told" and "the caller told it the wrong thing" looked identical. And these lines READ each global, so a
  ; parameter the caller forgot to set now fails HERE - before the tool has moved - naming itself, instead
  ; of aborting mid-probe (see the error:2 note in the header) or, worse, silently reading as something.
  ; Split across several PRINTs rather than one: grblHAL's line buffer is finite and a truncated trace is
  ; a trace you cannot trust. The ENTER line above is deliberately left untouched so older logs still diff.
  (PRINT, PC IN2 mode=#<mode> maxz=#<maxz> appz=#<appz> edgemargin=#<edge_margin>)
  (PRINT, PC IN3 bottom=#<_bottom> thickness=#<_ls_thickness> zfloor=#<_ls_zfloor>)
  (PRINT, PC IN4 plateoffset=#<_ls_plateoffset> lipoffset=#<_ls_lipoffset>)
  (PRINT, PC IN5 topx=#<_ls_topx> topy=#<_ls_topy>)
  (PRINT, PC IN6 searchf=#<_ls_searchf> latchf=#<_ls_latchf>)
  (PRINT, PC IN7 spacer=#<_ls_spacer> - accepted and ignored, see header)



  ; ================= stock-top Z at THIS corner (EVERY corner -> flatness) =================
  ; Move the fixture type's top-probe offset (#<_ls_topx>/#<_ls_topy>) into the stock interior (sign per
  ; corner) and probe the top, so each corner reports its own Z - the spread across the four corners is
  ; the stock/spoilboard flatness. #<_topx>/#<_topy> persist as the edge probes' anchor below.
  ; Pre-probe approach clearance above the floor (#<_bottom>): the caller's own best estimate of stock
  ; thickness plus, when running a touch plate, the plate's own thickness sitting on top of the stock - so
  ; this scales with what's ACTUALLY there instead of blindly assuming worst-case 1in (25.4mm) material
  ; regardless of real thickness. +10 is a fixed safety margin against estimate error (same spirit as the
  ; entered-width/height "estimate should be a few mm larger than actual" guidance elsewhere). Confirmed on
  ; real hardware 2026-07-27: with a 3/4in stock + 1/4in spacer + 12mm touch plate, the OLD blind "+30"
  ; barely cleared the stock and did not clear the touch plate at all - a real collision risk.
  #<_approach_z> = [#<_bottom> + #<_ls_thickness> + #<_ls_plateoffset> + 10]
  G90
  ; NE 0, not GT 0 (2026-07 bug, confirmed on real hardware on both this path and o43 below): #<maxz> is a
  ; MACHINE Z coordinate, and on a machine that homes Z to the top (Z0) with the whole work envelope below
  ; that, every real value here is NEGATIVE - "GT 0" silently failed for every legitimate maxz the caller
  ; ever passed (the vise's own #<_lv_safe_z>, Corner Fence's #<c1_maxz>), falling through to the ELSE
  ; branch every time with no error, just quietly slower/more conservative than intended.
  ;
  ; #<appz> (caller-optional, see file header) overrides ONLY this first move - everything else this call
  ; (the pre-top-probe descent below, the final retreat) still uses the caller's real #<maxz>, same as
  ; every other corner. Needed for a corner reached via a detour that left the fixture's footprint entirely
  ; (e.g. Corner Fence's TLO-reference park at G30, not verified clear of fence/clamp hardware) - #<maxz>
  ; is only trusted WITHIN the footprint (verified by an earlier corner's own measurement), so that one
  ; return crossing must happen at Z0 instead, without also giving up the trusted height for the rest of
  ; this corner's own probe sequence. Sentinel is 9999 ("unset"), NOT 0 - unlike #<maxz>, Z0 (machine top)
  ; is itself a legitimate override value here (that's the whole point), so 0 can't double as "no override".
  o48 IF [#<appz> LT 9000]
    G53 G0 Z[#<appz>]
  o48 ELSE
  o42 IF [#<maxz> NE 0]
    G53 G0 Z[#<maxz>]             ; rapid - caller already knows a trusted safe height (e.g. the vise's own centre probe) - never go any higher than necessary
  o42 ELSE
    G53 G0 Z0                     ; rapid - later corners: retract FULLY to machine top before crossing to the next corner - safer than just above the previous corner's stock in case there's clamp/fence hardware in between
  o42 ENDIF
  o48 ENDIF
  #<_topx> = [#<rx> + [#<_ls_topx> * #<sx>]]
  #<_topy> = [#<ry> + [#<_ls_topy> * #<sy>]]
  G53 G0 X[#<_topx>] Y[#<_topy>]    ; rapid - travelling AT the safe height set above
  ; Descend to a safe height before this corner's own top probe - NOT #<_safe_z>, which still holds the
  ; PREVIOUS corner's height (top+20) at this point in the code (this corner's own top probe, which
  ; recomputes it, hasn't run yet). Flatness/spacer variance between corners means the previous corner's
  ; safe height can sit below THIS corner's actual top - a bare G0 rapid to a stale height would drive
  ; straight into solid stock with nothing to catch it. If the caller already knows a trusted height
  ; (#<maxz>, e.g. the vise's own centre probe) use that directly; otherwise fall back to the same
  ; thickness/plate-aware #<_approach_z> computed above (same reasoning) -
  ; this is the branch a Corner Fence's OWN corner 1 actually takes (it has no #<c1_maxz> yet - that's
  ; only computed AFTER corner 1 finishes, for corners 2-4 to use), so this is exactly what needed fixing.
  o43 IF [#<maxz> NE 0]
    G53 G0 Z[#<maxz>]
  o43 ELSE
    G53 G0 Z[#<_approach_z>]
  o43 ENDIF
  ; SAFETY + early-out. The stock top always sits ABOVE the effective floor (spoilboard + spacer), so bound
  ; this seek instead of driving to a deep machine target - a dead/mis-wired probe then alarms near the board
  ; instead of ploughing to -140. Corners are placed from the ESTIMATED size, so cap them at the floor: a real corner triggers on the
  ; material above it, but a corner left over BARE board (estimate too big) reaches the floor with nothing
  ; there and G38.2 aborts right away - no doomed side search into air. The spacer band guarantees the cap
  ; clears the board (floor > spoilboard); with spacer 0 the +1 keeps it a hair above the board.
  ; ---- PROBE-RELEASED GATE ----
  ; grblHAL refuses a G38.2 whose probe is ALREADY triggered and reports a bare ALARM:4, which the
  ; caller then sees as "Warning: error 9 in named sub pcorner.macro" plus a triple error:9 cascade -
  ; three unrelated-looking failures naming nothing. Confirmed on real hardware 2026-08-16 with a
  ; failing 3D probe: it latched on the X face and stayed asserted through a 10 mm retract, a Z lift
  ; and a diagonal traverse, so the NEXT seek was refused before it started.
  ;
  ; #<_probe_state> is grblHAL's own hal.probe.is_triggered (ngc_params.c): 1 = triggered, 0 = clear,
  ; -1 = the driver cannot report it. FAIL OPEN on -1 - a controller that cannot answer must not lose
  ; the ability to probe - and abort with a sentence on 1, which is the whole point of this gate.
  o14 IF [#<_probe_state> EQ 1]
    (ABORT, Probe is still triggered before the top seek - it did not release. Check the probe, its wiring and $6/$I inversion.)
  o14 ENDIF
  G38.2 Z[#<_bottom> + 1] F[#<_ls_searchf>]        ; refuse to seek below the caller's material floor
  G91 G1 Z2 F1000
  G38.2 Z-5 F[#<_ls_latchf>]
  #<_z_top> = #5063
  #<_safe_z> = [#<_z_top> + 5]      ; safe travel height now known: 5 mm above this corner's top
  ; Repositioning within this corner (the X-face and Y-face retreats, and the exit retreat below) prefer the
  ; CALLER'S trusted #<maxz> (e.g. the operator-set Corner travel margin, or the vise's own centre probe) over
  ; freshly recomputing _safe_z - when the caller has one, it's a single, predictable height good for the
  ; whole run, not a per-corner value that changes call to call for no reason the operator can see. #<_safe_z>
  ; itself is still always computed (needed as the fallback here, and as what a LATER corner call reads via
  ; #<maxz> once it's been threaded back out by the caller).
  #<_retreat_z> = #<_safe_z>
  o44 IF [#<maxz> NE 0]
    #<_retreat_z> = #<maxz>
  o44 ENDIF
  G91 G1 Z10 F1000
  G90
  (PRINT, PC z_top=#<_z_top>)

  ; ================= start_z for the face probes (fixed 5mm below the measured top) =================
  ; Was top - thickness/2 (material midpoint) - changed to a fixed 5mm inset (2026-07-20, user request):
  ; simpler and independent of the caller's estimated #<_ls_thickness>, which is only ever an estimate
  ; before Measure has actually run. 5mm clears a top chamfer/rounded edge on any stock thick enough to
  ; probe at all (preconditions require >= 3mm... clamped up to the zfloor safety below regardless).
  #<_start_z> = [#<_z_top> - 5]
  ; SAFETY: never position the probe below the machine's Z soft-limit floor (#<_ls_zfloor>, passed by Load Stock
  ; from $132). When the stock sits low - its top near the Z travel bottom - start_z can fall below
  ; reachable Z, and the G53 G1 descent to it trips Alarm:2 (soft limit). Clamp up to the floor; the side face is
  ; still contacted since the face seek is horizontal from start_z.
  o32 IF [#<_start_z> LT #<_ls_zfloor>]
    #<_start_z> = #<_ls_zfloor>
  o32 ENDIF
  (PRINT, PC start_z=#<_start_z>)

  ; Fallback outputs for a face this call skips (faces=1/2, Dynamic mode edge-midpoint picks) - the
  ; un-probed axis reports the top-probe anchor, NOT a real touch. Overwritten below when that face's
  ; block actually runs.
  #<_corner_x> = #<_topx>
  #<_corner_y> = #<_topy>

  ; ================= X face probe (skipped when #<faces> EQ 2 - Y face only) =================
  o90 IF [#<faces> NE 2]
  ; From the top-probe position (over solid stock, verified above) move ONLY X back out to the reference
  ; (the face) - Y unchanged, so the pre-seek position is anchored to a point already known to be over
  ; material rather than a separately estimated offset. Descend to start_z, then seek toward the face.
  ; #<rx> alone (the fixture's saved reference) was calibrated to clear a 3D probe's BODY diameter - a touch
  ; plate's lip sticks out #<_ls_lipoffset> mm FURTHER than that, so the vertical descent right below needs
  ; the SAME extra outward push, or it can land right at (or on top of) the plate's own wall instead of in
  ; clear air. Subtracting [lipoffset + rad + edge_margin]*sx moves it outward (opposite sx's interior-pointing
  ; convention). rad+lipoffset alone is only exactly enough clearance if #<rx> sits EXACTLY at the true edge -
  ; but #<rx> is the caller's own estimate, not something this macro has verified yet (that's the whole point
  ; of the face probe about to run), so #<edge_margin> pads it - shooting blind at an unconfirmed edge needs
  ; slop, not a razor-thin geometric calculation. MUST include #<rad>, not just lipoffset: lipoffset alone only
  ; clears the plate with the bit's CENTER, not its full diameter. Confirmed on real hardware 2026-07-30 (an
  ; 8mm bit's center landed exactly on the plate's edge, half the bit still over it, and the descent to
  ; start_z caught the edge). The later G38.2 target is untouched (still an absolute #<rx> + 35*sx) - starting
  ; further out just means it travels a bit further to reach the same target.
  G53 G0 Z[#<_retreat_z>]           ; rapid - the trusted known-safe height when the caller has one, else just above this corner's own top (VERIFIED by the probe just run)
  G53 G0 X[#<rx> - [[#<_ls_lipoffset> + #<rad> + #<edge_margin>] * #<sx>]] Y[#<_topy>]       ; rapid - travelling AT the verified safe height
  G53 G0 Z[#<_z_top> + 12]          ; rapid down to within 12mm of the KNOWN top (just probed) - controlled feed only takes over for the final approach
  G53 G1 F1000 Z[#<_start_z>]       ; controlled - final descent to the face-probe depth
  ; ABSOLUTE seeks (not G91): a G91 move right after a G53 mis-computes on a $23-inverted axis
  ; (firmware bug - target sign-flips -> false Alarm:2). Current X is #<rx>, so seek to an absolute X.
  ; ---- PROBE-RELEASED GATE ----
  ; grblHAL refuses a G38.2 whose probe is ALREADY triggered and reports a bare ALARM:4, which the
  ; caller then sees as "Warning: error 9 in named sub pcorner.macro" plus a triple error:9 cascade -
  ; three unrelated-looking failures naming nothing. Confirmed on real hardware 2026-08-16 with a
  ; failing 3D probe: it latched on the X face and stayed asserted through a 10 mm retract, a Z lift
  ; and a diagonal traverse, so the NEXT seek was refused before it started.
  ;
  ; #<_probe_state> is grblHAL's own hal.probe.is_triggered (ngc_params.c): 1 = triggered, 0 = clear,
  ; -1 = the driver cannot report it. FAIL OPEN on -1 - a controller that cannot answer must not lose
  ; the ability to probe - and abort with a sentence on 1, which is the whole point of this gate.
  o15 IF [#<_probe_state> EQ 1]
    (ABORT, Probe is still triggered before the X face seek - it did not release. Check the probe, its wiring and $6/$I inversion.)
  o15 ENDIF
  G38.2 X[#<rx> + [35 * #<sx>]] F[#<_ls_searchf>]      ; coarse seek toward the X face
  #<x_c1> = #5061
  G1 X[#<x_c1> - [4 * #<sx>]] F1000                    ; back off so the deflecting ball RELEASES before the slow re-probe (G1: gentle, no rapid-accel jerk)
  G38.2 X[#<x_c1> + [6 * #<sx>]] F[#<_ls_latchf>]      ; slow re-probe (2 mm back-off left it stuck triggered -> Alarm:4; 4 mm clears it)
  #<x_trig> = #5061
  G1 X[#<x_trig> - [10 * #<sx>]] F1000
  G90
  (PRINT, PC x_trig=#<x_trig>)
  ; #<_ls_lipoffset> folds into the SAME term as the probe tip radius - both push the reported edge back
  ; toward the interior by a fixed amount, for the same reason (the probed surface isn't the true edge
  ; itself); 0 for the 3D probe, so this is a no-op there.
  #<_corner_x> = [#<x_trig> + [[#<rad> + #<_ls_lipoffset>] * #<sx>]]
  o90 ENDIF

  ; ================= Y face probe (skipped when #<faces> EQ 1 - X face only) =================
  o91 IF [#<faces> NE 1]
  ; Retract to a trusted safe height first - the X face probe left the tip near start_z (probing depth), so
  ; crossing at that depth would drag the tip across the stock top. Once at that height, go straight (X AND Y
  ; together) to the Y-face-start position (topx, ry) - no need to detour via the top-probe XY (topx, topy)
  ; first: that routing was only needed because the OLD retreat height was this corner's own freshly-computed
  ; _safe_z, not independently verified clear of the stock along an arbitrary diagonal. #<_retreat_z> (above)
  ; is either that same _safe_z or the caller's own trusted height - both are clear of the stock everywhere in
  ; the vicinity, not just directly above the last probed point, so a diagonal move at that height is exactly
  ; as safe as the old axis-at-a-time routing, just one move instead of two.
  ; Same lip+radius+margin clearance push as the X face start above - see its comment.
  G53 G0 Z[#<_retreat_z>]           ; rapid - verified/trusted safe height (see X face above)
  G53 G0 X[#<_topx>] Y[#<ry> - [[#<_ls_lipoffset> + #<rad> + #<edge_margin>] * #<sy>]]       ; rapid - diagonal, straight to the Y-face-start position, AT that height
  G53 G0 Z[#<_z_top> + 12]          ; rapid down to within 12mm of the KNOWN top (see X face above)
  G53 G1 F1000 Z[#<_start_z>]       ; controlled - final descent to the face-probe depth
  ; ABSOLUTE seeks (see X face) - current Y is #<ry>, so seek to an absolute Y.
  ; ---- PROBE-RELEASED GATE ----
  ; grblHAL refuses a G38.2 whose probe is ALREADY triggered and reports a bare ALARM:4, which the
  ; caller then sees as "Warning: error 9 in named sub pcorner.macro" plus a triple error:9 cascade -
  ; three unrelated-looking failures naming nothing. Confirmed on real hardware 2026-08-16 with a
  ; failing 3D probe: it latched on the X face and stayed asserted through a 10 mm retract, a Z lift
  ; and a diagonal traverse, so the NEXT seek was refused before it started.
  ;
  ; #<_probe_state> is grblHAL's own hal.probe.is_triggered (ngc_params.c): 1 = triggered, 0 = clear,
  ; -1 = the driver cannot report it. FAIL OPEN on -1 - a controller that cannot answer must not lose
  ; the ability to probe - and abort with a sentence on 1, which is the whole point of this gate.
  o16 IF [#<_probe_state> EQ 1]
    (ABORT, Probe is still triggered before the Y face seek - it did not release. Check the probe, its wiring and $6/$I inversion.)
  o16 ENDIF
  G38.2 Y[#<ry> + [35 * #<sy>]] F[#<_ls_searchf>]      ; coarse seek toward the Y face
  #<y_c1> = #5062
  G1 Y[#<y_c1> - [4 * #<sy>]] F1000                    ; back off before the slow re-probe (G1: gentle, no rapid-accel jerk)
  G38.2 Y[#<y_c1> + [6 * #<sy>]] F[#<_ls_latchf>]      ; slow re-probe
  #<y_trig> = #5062
  G1 Y[#<y_trig> - [10 * #<sy>]] F1000
  G90
  (PRINT, PC y_trig=#<y_trig>)
  ; #<_ls_lipoffset> folds into the SAME term as the probe tip radius - both push the reported edge back
  ; toward the interior by a fixed amount, for the same reason (the probed surface isn't the true edge
  ; itself); 0 for the 3D probe, so this is a no-op there.
  #<_corner_y> = [#<y_trig> + [[#<rad> + #<_ls_lipoffset>] * #<sy>]]
  o91 ENDIF

  ; ================= outputs =================
  #<_corner_z> = [#<_z_top> - #<_ls_plateoffset>]   ; 0 for the 3D probe and touch-plate-on-conductive-stock (no-op)
  (PRINT, PC OUT c=#<corner> x=#<_corner_x> y=#<_corner_y> z=#<_corner_z>)

  ; retract before returning to the caller - the next corner travels at this height. Rapid: #<_retreat_z> is
  ; either a VERIFIED height (20mm above this corner's just-probed top) or the caller's own trusted one, so
  ; this retract only ever moves AWAY from material, never toward it.
  G53 G0 Z[#<_retreat_z>]
  (PRINT, PC EXIT)
O<pcorner> ENDSUB
